    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a more detailed explanation of mitosis, incorporating the phases and using the Lego analogy to help students understand the process:\n\n\"Mitosis is a crucial process that occurs in all eukaryotic cells, including our own skin cells. It's a type of cell division that involves a series of precise steps, kind of like following a set of instructions to build a Lego castle. Each stage is carefully orchestrated to ensure that the new cells produced are identical to the parent cell.\n\n**Prophase**: In this first stage, the genetic material, or DNA, in the cell gets prepared for division. The chromatin, the complex of DNA and proteins, condenses into visible chromosomes, making it easier to see during cell division. It's like sorting and preparing the Lego pieces before building the castle.\n\n**Metaphase**: Next, the chromosomes line up in the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers, which act like tiny Lego tracks. This ensures that each new cell will receive an identical set of chromosomes. The chromosomes pair up with each other, much like how Lego partners to build a sturdy structure.\n\n**Anaphase**: Now, the sister chromatids (the paired chromosomes) separate, moving to opposite poles of the cell.",
      "source": "target",
